  "issue_number": 297,
  "issue_title": "[bug] extract_choice_letter misses a Markdown-emphasised choice letter (`**B**`, `*E*`), a false-negative grade",
  "issue_body": "**Describe the bug**\n\n`extract_choice_letter` (`src/trinity/orchestration/reward.py`) misses a multiple-choice letter wrapped in **Markdown emphasis** (`**B**`, `*E*`, `__A__`, `` `F` ``), producing a false-negative grade on MMLU / GPQA / MMLU-Pro.\n\nThe extractor unwraps the LaTeX equivalent (`\\textbf{B}`, `\\text{B}`) via `_strip_choice_font_wrappers`, but nothing strips Markdown emphasis. So for `The answer is **B**.`, the commitment pattern `answer\\s*(?:is|:)?\\s*\\(?\\s*([A-J])...` cannot cross the `*` characters between `is` and `B`, and no letter is captured.\n\n**Why it matters \u2014 and why it's uncontestable**\n\nBolding the final answer letter is one of the most common ways a model formats its answer, so this is a frequent false negative that feeds both `is_correct` (eval accuracy) and `training_reward`.\n\nThe codebase already treats Markdown emphasis as noise everywhere else this kind of token is parsed:\n\n- `src/trinity/roles/verifier.py:47` \u2014 `VERDICT_RE = re.compile(r\"VERDICT\\b[\\s:*_`~-]*(ACCEPT|REVISE)...\")` deliberately tolerates `*`, `_`, `` ` `` between the keyword and the committed token, with the comment *\"Models routinely format the line as `**VERDICT:** ACCEPT`\"*.\n- `extract_choice_letter` itself already unwraps the **LaTeX** emphasis equivalent (`\\textbf{B}`).\n\nSo the Markdown case is simply an inconsistency: the verifier tolerates `**VERDICT:** ACCEPT`, and the choice extractor tolerates `\\textbf{B}`, but the choice extractor rejects the far more common `The answer is **B**`.\n\n**Expected vs actual**\n\n```python\nextract_choice_letter(\"The answer is **B**.\")   # actual: None    expected: \"B\"\nscore_text(\"mmlu\", \"The answer is **B**.\", \"B\")  # actual: 0.0     expected: 1.0  (false negative)\n```\n\nSame failure for `\"Answer: **D**\"`, `\"The answer is *E*\"`, `\"answer is __A__\"`, `` \"The answer is `F`\" ``.\n\n**To reproduce** (no GPU/network):\n\n```python\nimport sys; sys.path.insert(0, \"src\")\nfrom trinity.orchestration import reward as R\nprint(R.extract_choice_letter(\"The answer is **B**.\"))          # -> None\nprint(R.score_text(\"mmlu\", \"The answer is **B**.\", \"B\"))        # -> 0.0\n```\n\n**Suggested fix**\n\nMirror `_strip_choice_font_wrappers`: before matching, strip a **paired** Markdown emphasis marker wrapping exactly one `A\u2013J` letter. A backreference requires the same marker on both sides, and word-boundary guards keep it from touching `snake_case` identifiers or longer marker runs:\n\n```python\nre.compile(r\"(?<![\\w*])(\\*\\*|\\*|__|_|`)([A-Ja-j])\\1(?![\\w*])\")\n```\n\nThis only rewrites `<marker>LETTER<marker>` \u2192 `LETTER`, so any other text is byte-identical. I verified it fixes the cases above while leaving every existing choice/grading test unchanged, and that it does **not** turn `MAX_A_VAL`, `A_B_C`, an echoed option list, or emphasis around a *non-committed* letter into a spurious answer.\n\n**Environment**\n- Python version: `Python 3.10.12`\n- GPU: CPU only (repro needs neither GPU nor network)\n- Commit hash: `b647478`\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-15T06:12:53.000Z",
